WEBVTT

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.143
These grains are holding up a dam.

00:00:06.493 --> 00:00:09.512
Something is pushing them
sideways, very slowly.

00:00:09.862 --> 00:00:11.302
Nobody is shaking them.

00:00:11.652 --> 00:00:14.732
Watch the lines between
the grains: they show

00:00:14.732 --> 00:00:16.899
how hard the grains press on each other.

00:00:17.249 --> 00:00:18.085
They're fading.

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:23.822
In twenty nineteen, in Brazil, a dam
standing on grains like these collapsed.

00:00:24.172 --> 00:00:27.609
It took under ten seconds,
and there was no earthquake.

00:00:27.959 --> 00:00:31.070
Within weeks, Brazil banned
the way it was built.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:35.507
Chile had turned against the same way
of building half a century before.

00:00:35.857 --> 00:00:37.297
Why that one way?

00:00:39.647 --> 00:00:43.222
A mine grinds rock to powder
to get the metal out.

00:00:43.572 --> 00:00:47.195
What's left is ground rock
and water, called tailings.

00:00:47.545 --> 00:00:53.118
It's piped out as a slurry, like runny
grey mud, and kept behind a dam for good.

00:00:53.468 --> 00:00:56.347
To see why that's hard,
go back to the beach.

00:00:56.697 --> 00:00:58.322
Dry sand pours.

00:00:58.672 --> 00:01:01.505
Damp sand stands up as a castle wall.

00:01:01.855 --> 00:01:07.428
But at the water's edge, soaked sand turns
to soup: wiggle your feet, and they sink.

00:01:07.778 --> 00:01:10.332
That's the idea behind this whole series.

00:01:10.682 --> 00:01:13.933
Soil is strong because its
grains press on each other.

00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:18.091
Water in the gaps pushes them apart,
and takes that strength away.

00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:19.695
Remember the soup.

00:01:20.945 --> 00:01:24.010
A water dam is finished
the day it's built.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.217
A tailings dam never is.

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:29.347
The mill sends more
every day, for as long as

00:01:29.347 --> 00:01:32.094
the mine runs, so the
dam has to keep growing.

00:01:32.444 --> 00:01:34.673
It starts as a small starter dam.

00:01:35.023 --> 00:01:38.645
Pipes along its top, the
crest, pour the slurry out.

00:01:38.995 --> 00:01:42.850
The coarse grains settle near
the crest, and build a beach.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.240
The fine grains and the
water run on, to a pond.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:51.816
When it's nearly full, the dam is
raised, and then raised again.

00:01:52.166 --> 00:01:55.881
There are three ways to do
it, from the same starter dam.

00:01:56.231 --> 00:02:00.643
Upstream: each new wall steps back
toward the pond, onto the beach.

00:02:00.993 --> 00:02:03.361
It's often built from
the beach's own sand.

00:02:03.711 --> 00:02:06.173
Centreline: each one goes straight up.

00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:11.631
Downstream: each one steps away from the
pond, over the dam's own outer slope.

00:02:12.881 --> 00:02:14.460
Here's a drawing from the course.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.129
The starter dam is twenty metres
tall, and it's raised four times.

00:02:19.479 --> 00:02:22.683
Upstream, every raise
is the same small wall.

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:26.273
Downstream, every raise has
to cover the whole outer

00:02:26.273 --> 00:02:29.163
face, so each one is bigger than the last.

00:02:29.513 --> 00:02:33.229
In all, downstream takes about
four times the fill of upstream.

00:02:33.579 --> 00:02:38.037
Its last raise alone takes eight
times as much as an upstream one.

00:02:38.387 --> 00:02:43.031
Make a dam like this twice as tall, and
it takes nearly four times the fill.

00:02:43.381 --> 00:02:45.285
Centreline sits in between.

00:02:45.635 --> 00:02:48.654
Fill means trucks, time and money.

00:02:49.004 --> 00:02:52.533
So upstream is the cheapest
way, and the quickest.

00:02:52.883 --> 00:02:55.205
Why would anyone build any other way?

00:02:56.455 --> 00:02:59.835
Here's the idea most people
start with: a dam fails

00:02:59.835 --> 00:03:02.957
when its wall breaks, so
build the wall strong.

00:03:03.307 --> 00:03:06.279
But look at what an
upstream wall stands on.

00:03:06.629 --> 00:03:09.833
It isn't on rock, or on the old dam.

00:03:10.183 --> 00:03:14.243
Its crest has walked toward the
pond, and most of the dam now sits

00:03:14.243 --> 00:03:18.264
on the beach: tailings laid down
by the pipes, years before.

00:03:18.614 --> 00:03:21.911
On top, that beach can look like
the damp sand of the castle.

00:03:22.261 --> 00:03:25.001
But inside the dam, there's a water level.

00:03:25.351 --> 00:03:28.416
And below it, the tailings
are loose, and soaked.

00:03:28.766 --> 00:03:29.973
That's the soup.

00:03:30.323 --> 00:03:33.388
So what does the soup do,
when something pushes on it?

00:03:34.638 --> 00:03:38.864
This is the course's grain model,
a teaching model in two dimensions.

00:03:39.214 --> 00:03:41.025
Each disc is a grain.

00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:44.023
These are loose, with water in every gap.

00:03:44.373 --> 00:03:47.391
On top, a weight,
standing in for the wall.

00:03:47.741 --> 00:03:51.874
At the bottom, a drain, shut,
so the water can't get out.

00:03:52.224 --> 00:03:54.500
Now push it sideways, slowly.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.289
No shaking at all.

00:03:56.639 --> 00:04:00.540
Will the grains pack down and
hold, like sand under a footstep?

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:04.087
They don't.

00:04:04.437 --> 00:04:08.199
The box hardly gets any shorter,
and the water takes over.

00:04:08.549 --> 00:04:12.309
The bar is the weight on
the box: amber is the share

00:04:12.309 --> 00:04:15.561
the grains carry, blue the
share the water carries.

00:04:15.911 --> 00:04:19.348
Before long, the water is
carrying about nine tenths of it.

00:04:19.698 --> 00:04:23.877
The grains hardly press on each
other, and most of the lines fade.

00:04:24.227 --> 00:04:25.110
Nothing broke.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.317
The grains just stopped pressing.

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:30.927
That's liquefaction: the
grains lose their press,

00:04:30.927 --> 00:04:33.704
and the soil flows like a heavy liquid.

00:04:34.954 --> 00:04:37.044
So why does the water take over?

00:04:37.394 --> 00:04:40.134
Open the drain, and give the same push.

00:04:40.484 --> 00:04:45.267
This time the sand settles, just a
little, and the grains keep pressing.

00:04:45.617 --> 00:04:50.401
Pushed, loose grains shuffle into the gaps
between them, and try to pack tighter.

00:04:50.751 --> 00:04:55.209
With the drain open, the water squeezed
out of those gaps simply leaves.

00:04:55.559 --> 00:04:58.438
With the drain shut, it has nowhere to go.

00:04:58.788 --> 00:05:03.850
So the grains can't move closer, and the
weight passes from the grains to the water.

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:07.219
Now try dense grains, with the drain shut.

00:05:07.569 --> 00:05:10.309
Pushed, they have to climb
over each other, so the

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:13.374
gaps grow, and the water
in them is pulled tight.

00:05:13.724 --> 00:05:18.693
That pull draws the grains together, and
they press more than three times as hard.

00:05:19.043 --> 00:05:20.900
It has to be loose enough, though.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.223
In the model, only the
loosest packings let go.

00:05:24.573 --> 00:05:26.848
Pack them a little tighter, and they hold.

00:05:27.198 --> 00:05:31.478
That's why a downstream raise is
built of compacted fill, rolled

00:05:31.478 --> 00:05:34.768
dense in layers, with drains
inside, and stands on it.

00:05:35.118 --> 00:05:37.858
A centreline raise
stands mostly on it too.

00:05:39.108 --> 00:05:41.244
But isn't liquefaction
an earthquake thing?

00:05:41.594 --> 00:05:44.520
In nineteen sixty-five, an
earthquake shook Chile.

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.850
The El Cobre tailings
dams, raised the upstream

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:51.000
way, liquefied, and
buried the village below.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.718
More than two hundred people were killed.

00:05:54.068 --> 00:05:57.628
In nineteen seventy, Chile's
first rule for tailings dams

00:05:57.628 --> 00:06:01.174
allowed only the other two
ways, where people live below.

00:06:01.524 --> 00:06:05.564
Shake our box, and it does the
same: the grains stop pressing.

00:06:06.814 --> 00:06:10.065
But Brumadinho needed no earthquake.

00:06:10.415 --> 00:06:16.359
Dam One, at an iron ore mine near the town,
was raised the upstream way, ten times.

00:06:16.709 --> 00:06:19.329
On the twenty-fifth of
January, twenty nineteen,

00:06:19.329 --> 00:06:21.585
no earthquake was recorded in the region.

00:06:21.935 --> 00:06:26.347
Cameras were watching it, and until the
moment it went, nothing looked wrong.

00:06:26.697 --> 00:06:29.390
Its slope collapsed in under ten seconds.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.874
Within five minutes, about three
quarters of its tailings had flowed out.

00:06:34.224 --> 00:06:36.546
Two hundred and seventy
people were killed.

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:39.728
And no one had raised it
for more than five years.

00:06:40.078 --> 00:06:43.561
The growing had stopped, but
the soup was still inside.

00:06:43.911 --> 00:06:48.277
A tailings dam has to stand long
after the mine has closed, for good.

00:06:48.627 --> 00:06:51.088
Investigations differ on what set it off.

00:06:51.438 --> 00:06:55.664
They agree on what happened: the
tailings in the dam itself liquefied.

00:06:56.014 --> 00:06:58.011
The wall didn't break first.

00:06:58.361 --> 00:07:01.751
It stood on the soup, and the soup let go.

00:07:03.001 --> 00:07:05.462
So here's why that one way was banned.

00:07:05.812 --> 00:07:08.599
An upstream raise stands on the soup.

00:07:08.949 --> 00:07:12.107
And a slow push is enough
to make the soup let go.

00:07:15.457 --> 00:07:18.290
At the beach, the soup
lets your feet sink.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:21.983
Under an upstream raise, it
can let the whole dam go.

00:07:22.333 --> 00:07:25.213
The next year, Brazil
wrote the ban into law.

00:07:25.563 --> 00:07:28.103
It defines the banned
method just like that:

00:07:28.103 --> 00:07:30.625
walls resting on the tailings themselves.

00:07:31.875 --> 00:07:33.686
Brumadinho wasn't the first.

00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:37.216
Stava, in Italy, where an
upper dam fell onto the

00:07:37.216 --> 00:07:40.305
one below it: two hundred
and sixty-eight killed.

00:07:40.655 --> 00:07:44.175
Merriespruit, in South Africa,
where a dam overtopped in a

00:07:44.175 --> 00:07:47.714
storm, and its tailings flowed
through a suburb: seventeen.

00:07:48.064 --> 00:07:50.990
Fundão, in Brazil: nineteen.

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:54.220
Fundão's tailings ran down
the rivers for more than

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.377
six hundred kilometres, all
the way to the Atlantic.

00:07:57.727 --> 00:07:59.445
Each had its own trigger.

00:07:59.795 --> 00:08:02.024
All of them were raised the upstream way.

00:08:03.274 --> 00:08:05.457
So is the trouble mine waste?

00:08:05.807 --> 00:08:09.987
Coquitlam Dam, in British
Columbia, holds water,

00:08:09.987 --> 00:08:12.216
some of it Vancouver's drinking water.

00:08:12.566 --> 00:08:15.126
It was built more than a
century ago by hydraulic

00:08:15.126 --> 00:08:18.138
fill: soil washed into place with water.

00:08:18.488 --> 00:08:22.111
That leaves it loose and
wet, like an upstream raise.

00:08:22.461 --> 00:08:26.408
Engineers found a zone in it that
could liquefy in a big earthquake.

00:08:26.758 --> 00:08:29.978
So BC Hydro built a new
dam of compacted earth

00:08:29.978 --> 00:08:32.888
and rock, against the old one's outer toe.

00:08:33.238 --> 00:08:35.978
What matters isn't water or tailings.

00:08:36.328 --> 00:08:39.579
It's loose and wet, or dense and drained.

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:44.629
It's the series' idea once more:
soil holds while its grains press

00:08:44.629 --> 00:08:47.824
on each other, and water in
the gaps can take that away.

00:08:49.074 --> 00:08:52.214
Mount Polley, also in British
Columbia, was a tailings

00:08:52.214 --> 00:08:54.925
dam with a core, a filter and rockfill.

00:08:55.275 --> 00:08:57.318
It wasn't raised the upstream way.

00:08:57.668 --> 00:08:59.990
In twenty fourteen, it failed anyway.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:01.455
What gave way?
